# openvela ESP32-P4 AI Coding Guide

## Scope

This workspace ports openvela/NuttX to the ESP32-P4-Function-EV-Board. Keep changes focused on the requested subsystem and preserve unrelated worktree changes.

## Important paths

- openvela tree: `nuttx/`, `apps/`, `frameworks/`, `vendor/`
- Board delivery: `vendor/openvela/boards/contest2026_284_board/`
- Editable board source mirror: `contest2026_284_zhuanqianmaibanzi/board/contest_board/`
- ESP32-P4 HAL staged by the build: `nuttx/arch/risc-v/src/chip/esp-hal-3rdparty/`
- Pinned local HAL cache: `../local/esp-hal-3rdparty/`
- AI skill source: `.claude/skills/`
- Codex adapters: `.agents/skills/`

Some board-delivery files and editable board files may be hard links. Check inode identity before applying duplicate edits.

## Authoritative references

- Espressif BSP: https://github.com/espressif/esp-bsp
- ESP32-P4 Function EV Board BSP: `bsp/esp32_p4_function_ev_board/` in esp-bsp
- openvela AI skills: https://github.com/open-vela/.claude/tree/dev-ai-contest-2026

Use esp-bsp for board pin assignments, peripheral topology, reset/power sequencing, and known-good hardware behavior. Do not copy ESP-IDF driver code directly into NuttX: adapt it to NuttX upper-half/lower-half, VFS, IRQ, locking, and Kconfig conventions.

## Skills

- For openvela build, configuration, driver, review, size, memory, audio, PR, or contest-log tasks, use `.agents/skills/openvela-ai-skills/SKILL.md`. It routes to the relevant source skill under `.claude/skills/`.
- For ESP32-P4 board bring-up or peripheral work, use `.agents/skills/esp32p4-openvela-bsp/SKILL.md`.
- When a routed source skill names a Claude-only tool, use the equivalent available Codex capability. Do not install or assume an MCP dependency unless the user requests it.

## Build invariants

- Keep `esp-hal-3rdparty` pinned to the revision selected by `nuttx/arch/risc-v/src/chip/espressif/Make.defs`; do not mix `release/master.c`, `sync/master.c`, or arbitrary master revisions.
- Apply the board compatibility patch in `chips/esp32p4/patches/` when staging a clean HAL manually.
- Treat stale generated headers and objects as suspect after changing HAL or Kconfig baselines.
- `configs/nsh` is the minimal recovery baseline. `configs/velapoka` adds the project BSP and devices such as `/dev/velapoka` and GT911 `/dev/input0`.
- Build from this directory:

```bash
./build.sh vendor/openvela/boards/contest2026_284_board/configs/nsh -j2
./build.sh vendor/openvela/boards/contest2026_284_board/configs/velapoka -j2
```

## Verification

For minimal NSH, verify at least `help`, `uname -a`, increasing `uptime` and `/proc/uptime`, and that `sleep 2` returns. A visible `nsh>` alone is insufficient.

The simple-boot image is `nuttx/vela_nuttx.bin`, flashed at `0x2000`. `/dev/ttyACM*` is normally USB Serial/JTAG for flashing; `/dev/ttyUSB*` is normally the 115200 8N1 NSH UART. Resolve actual ports before use. Prefer host-side flashing if a VM drops USB devices during reset.

Never claim a peripheral is working only because its HAL source exists. Require the expected NuttX device node and an appropriate real-device smoke test.
